1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,880
Your jaw drops, your lungs fill up,

2
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:03,820
and for about six seconds you can't really stop

3
00:00:03,820 --> 00:00:05,980
it. But it's not because your body needs more

4
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:09,440
oxygen. You see, when scientists gave people extra oxygen

5
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,960
to breathe, they kept yawning just as much.

6
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:14,500
Instead, a yawn pulls in cool air and pumps

7
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:15,720
fresh blood to your head,

8
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,200
which some scientists think cools your brain down like

9
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,260
a radiator. That's why people yawn less when the

10
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:23,940
air outside is hotter than their body.

11
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:27,120
And animals with bigger brains have longer yawns.

12
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,020
But the strangest part is that just reading about

13
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:31,920
yawning can make you yawn.

14
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,800
So you've probably yawned at least once by now.
